{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c43b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07750-3853-73c0-b383-0773367c2b50"},"sequence":120,"recorded_at":1780531400000235,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb"},"call_id":"call_01a077506fa773f2b1d72b657e91af04","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/open_member_unix.go`.\n1|//go:build !windows\n2|\n3|package secprim\n4|\n5|import (\n6|\t\"errors\"\n7|\t\"os\"\n8|\n9|\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n10|)\n11|\n12|// openCommitRelative walks one validated member from the staging-root\n13|// directory handle to an open file descriptor without ever re-resolving\n14|// a path string: every component is opened relative to the previous\n15|// component's descriptor with openat. Intermediate components carry\n16|// O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY, so a symlinked directory on the path fails\n17|// with ELOOP instead of redirecting the walk outside the root; the\n18|// final component carries O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK, so a trailing symlink\n19|// fails with ELOOP and a FIFO returns immediately instead of stalling\n20|// the opener until a writer appears. Descriptors pin what they opened:\n21|// a rename or swap after any step cannot retarget a descriptor the\n22|// walk already holds, which is what closes the validate-then-open\n23|// TOCTOU rather than repeating it with more steps.\n24|//\n25|// The walk reports no refusal of its own: ELOOP maps to the shared\n26|// errCommitSymlink signal (refused as \"member symlink escape\" by the\n27|// caller) and every other failure returns raw (refused as \"open\n28|// failed\" with its cause). Shape checks on the final descriptor stay\n29|// with the caller, which stats the descriptor rather than the path.\n30|func openCommitRelative(root *os.File, _ string, member string, segments []string) (*os.File, error) {\n31|\tprevious := -1\n32|\tcurrent := int(root.Fd())\n33|\tfor _, segment := range segments[:len(segments)-1] {\n34|\t\tnext, err := unix.Openat(current, segment,\n35|\t\t\tunix.O_RDONLY|unix.O_NOFOLLOW|unix.O_DIRECTORY|unix.O_CLOEXEC, 0)\n36|\t\tif err != nil {\n37|\t\t\tif previous >= 0 {\n38|\t\t\t\t_ = unix.Close(previous)\n39|\t\t\t}\n40|\t\t\treturn nil, classifyCommitErr(current, segment, err)\n41|\t\t}\n42|\t\tif previous >= 0 {\n43|\t\t\t_ = unix.Close(previous)\n44|\t\t}\n45|\t\tprevious, current = next, next\n46|\t}\n47|\tlast := segments[len(segments)-1]\n48|\tfinal, err := unix.Openat(current, last,\n49|\t\tunix.O_RDONLY|unix.O_NOFOLLOW|unix.O_CLOEXEC|unix.O_NONBLOCK, 0)\n50|\tif previous >= 0 {\n51|\t\t_ = unix.Close(previous)\n52|\t}\n53|\tif err != nil {\n54|\t\treturn nil, classifyCommitErr(current, last, err)\n55|\t}\n56|\treturn os.NewFile(uintptr(final), member), nil\n57|}\n58|\n59|// classifyCommitErr sorts the walk failures: a symlink anywhere on the\n60|// path becomes the escape signal, while a missing component, a file\n61|// where a directory stands, or a permission failure stays raw for the\n62|// caller's open-failed refusal.\n63|//\n64|// The errno alone does not name the shape on every kernel: Linux\n65|// reports ELOOP for an O_NOFOLLOW openat meeting a symlink, while\n66|// Darwin reports ENOTDIR for the O_DIRECTORY half of the same event.\n67|// Either errno therefore re-examines the component with fstatat at\n68|// AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW — still relative to the parent descriptor, so\n69|// no path string is re-resolved — and only a component that is a\n70|// symlink right now becomes the escape signal. A swap between the\n71|// failed open and this check fails closed in every direction: each\n72|// outcome (symlink, directory, missing) refuses, and only the rule\n73|// name differs.\n74|func classifyCommitErr(parent int, segment string, err error) error {\n75|\tif errors.Is(err, unix.ELOOP) {\n76|\t\treturn errCommitSymlink\n77|\t}\n78|\tif errors.Is(err, unix.ENOTDIR) {\n79|\t\tvar status unix.Stat_t\n80|\t\tif fstatErr := unix.Fstatat(parent, segment, &status, unix.AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW); fstatErr == nil {\n81|\t\t\tif status.Mode&unix.S_IFMT == unix.S_IFLNK {\n82|\t\t\t\treturn errCommitSymlink\n83|\t\t\t}\n84|\t\t}\n85|\t}\n86|\treturn err\n87|}\n88|\n89|// clearNonblock drops O_NONBLOCK from an admitted regular file so a\n90|// borrowed descriptor never leaks the flag to its reader. Regular-file\n91|// reads ignore the flag either way.\n92|func clearNonblock(file *os.File) error {\n93|\treturn unix.SetNonblock(int(file.Fd()), false)\n94|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c43d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07750-3853-73c0-b383-0773367c2b50"},"sequence":121,"recorded_at":1780531400000237,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb"},"call_id":"call_01a07750704674f3a4f1b62c0f92c0bc","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/open_member_windows.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"os\"\n5|\t\"path/filepath\"\n6|\t\"syscall\"\n7|)\n8|\n9|// openCommitRelative is the Windows best effort for the guard commit\n10|// walk: Go has no openat, so each component is Lstat-checked against\n11|// the root-joined path before the final open. A symlink or reparse\n12|// point on the path returns the shared errCommitSymlink signal, which\n13|// the caller refuses as \"member symlink escape\".\n14|//\n15|// Stated bounds. The check-then-open race remains: a swap between the\n16|// component Lstat and the final open is not detected, and staging\n17|// roots being owner-only directories only narrows that window to the\n18|// owner. The walk additionally cannot see through the handle the way\n19|// the unix walk does: the root handle is validated as a directory by\n20|// the caller, but the component descent re-resolves the root path\n21|// string. Windows behavior in this package is compile-and-vet only,\n22|// never executed.\n23|//\n24|// Reparse coverage. The Lstat gate refuses ModeSymlink (true symlinks:\n25|// Go 1.23+ sets the bit only for IO_REPARSE_TAG_SYMLINK) and\n26|// ModeIrregular (every other reparse point — junctions, mount points,\n27|// AppExecLinks, cloud placeholders — which Go maps to ModeIrregular,\n28|// never to ModeSymlink). A junction-to-directory therefore refuses\n29|// here rather than passing the directory check the way a\n30|// symlink-only gate would admit it.\n31|func openCommitRelative(root *os.File, rootPath string, _ string, segments []string) (*os.File, error) {\n32|\t_ = root\n33|\tcurrent := rootPath\n34|\tfor _, segment := range segments[:len(segments)-1] {\n35|\t\tcurrent = filepath.Join(current, segment)\n36|\t\tinfo, err := os.Lstat(current)\n37|\t\tif err != nil {\n38|\t\t\treturn nil, err\n39|\t\t}\n40|\t\tif info.Mode()&(os.ModeSymlink|os.ModeIrregular) != 0 {\n41|\t\t\treturn nil, errCommitSymlink\n42|\t\t}\n43|\t\tif !info.IsDir() {\n44|\t\t\treturn nil, syscall.ENOTDIR\n45|\t\t}\n46|\t}\n47|\tfull := filepath.Join(rootPath, filepath.Join(segments...))\n48|\tinfo, err := os.Lstat(full)\n49|\tif err != nil {\n50|\t\treturn nil, err\n51|\t}\n52|\tif info.Mode()&(os.ModeSymlink|os.ModeIrregular) != 0 {\n53|\t\treturn nil, errCommitSymlink\n54|\t}\n55|\treturn os.OpenFile(full, os.O_RDONLY, 0)\n56|}\n57|\n58|// clearNonblock is inert on Windows: the Windows open sets no\n59|// non-blocking flag.\n60|func clearNonblock(_ *os.File) error {\n61|\treturn nil\n62|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c43f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07750-3853-73c0-b383-0773367c2b50"},"sequence":122,"recorded_at":1780531400000239,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb"},"call_id":"call_01a0775070bd7801b925c9a73c9724d5","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/nofollow.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"errors\"\n5|\t\"os\"\n6|\t\"strings\"\n7|)\n8|\n9|// errIsSymlink reports the Windows Lstat gate refusal. It stays internal:\n10|// callers see the failPath refusal, and errors.Is against ErrUnsafePath\n11|// is the supported match.\n12|var errIsSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink refused before open\")\n13|\n14|// errCommitSymlink is the internal signal from the platform commit walk\n15|// (openCommitRelative) that a symlink or reparse point stands on the\n16|// member path at commit time. It stays internal: Open maps it to the\n17|// failPath \"member symlink escape\" refusal, and errors.Is against\n18|// ErrUnsafePath is the supported match. The walk returns no failPath\n19|// sites of its own so the refusal inventory stays in shared code,\n20|// which the suite executes on every platform it runs on.\n21|var errCommitSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink on the commit path\")\n22|\n23|// OpenNoFollowFile opens an existing regular file read-only, refusing a\n24|// trailing symlink instead of traversing it (Section 16.3\n25|// \"symlink/reparse escape during both validation and commit\"). The\n26|// descriptor refers to the opened inode, and the post-open Fstat below\n27|// reads that descriptor rather than the path, so a swap between open and\n28|// stat cannot retarget the check: only the final-component open itself\n29|// races, and on unix O_NOFOLLOW closes even that.\n30|//\n31|// The file must be regular: directories, devices, pipes, and sockets are\n32|// refused after opening (and the descriptor closed) rather than handed\n33|// back. Parent directories are the caller's responsibility: open the\n34|// staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and resolve members through a Guard.\n35|func OpenNoFollowFile(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n36|\tfile, err := openNoFollow(path)\n37|\tif err != nil {\n38|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n39|\t}\n40|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(file)\n41|\tif err != nil {\n42|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n43|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n44|\t}\n45|\tif err := CheckRegularTarget(info); err != nil {\n46|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n47|\t\treturn nil, err\n48|\t}\n49|\t// The unix open sets O_NONBLOCK so a FIFO can never stall the opener\n50|\t// (its refusal above runs only after the open returns). A regular\n51|\t// file ignores O_NONBLOCK on reads, and clearing it here keeps a\n52|\t// borrowed descriptor from leaking the flag to its reader. fcntl on\n53|\t// a just-opened valid descriptor cannot fail; the best-effort clear\n54|\t// carries no refusal site for that reason.\n55|\t_ = clearNonblock(file)\n56|\treturn file, nil\n57|}\n58|\n59|// Open commits one guard member to an open regular file through the\n60|// staging-root directory handle, refusing symlink and reparse escape at\n61|// commit time (Section 16.3 \"symlink/reparse escape during both\n62|// validation and commit\").\n63|//\n64|// Validation and commit are two halves of one entry point, not two calls\n65|// the caller composes: the member grammar and the managed set refuse\n66|// lexically first, then the platform walk (openCommitRelative) opens\n67|// every path component relative to a directory descriptor — never by\n68|// re-resolving a path string — so a symlinked intermediate directory\n69|// cannot redirect the commit outside the root the way composing\n70|// Guard.Resolve with OpenNoFollowFile does (O_NOFOLLOW constrains only\n71|// the final component; every component before it is still followed by\n72|// the kernel when a path string is opened).\n73|//\n74|// The caller opens the staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and passes the\n75|// handle: on unix the walk never leaves that handle (openat from the\n76|// root descriptor down, O_NOFOLLOW per component, TOCTOU-closed); on\n77|// Windows the walk is a best-effort Lstat component check with the\n78|// stated check-then-open bound. The returned file is cleared of\n79|// O_NONBLOCK like OpenNoFollowFile. The caller owns closing both the\n80|// root handle and the returned file.\n81|func (guard Guard) Open(root *os.File, member string) (*os.File, error) {\n82|\tif err := CheckMemberPath(guard.platform, member); err != nil {\n83|\t\treturn nil, err\n84|\t}\n85|\tif guard.checkManaged && !guard.managed[member] {\n86|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"member unmanaged\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n87|\t}\n88|\tif root == nil {\n89|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"guard root handle\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n90|\t}\n91|\tif info, err := root.Stat(); err != nil || info == nil || !info.IsDir() {\n92|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"guard root handle\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n93|\t}\n94|\tsegments := strings.Split(member, \"/\")\n95|\tfile, err := openCommitRelative(root, guard.root, member, segments)\n96|\tif err != nil {\n97|\t\tif errors.Is(err, errCommitSymlink) {\n98|\t\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"member symlink escape\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n99|\t\t}\n100|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(member)).WithCause(err)\n101|\t}\n102|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(file)\n103|\tif err != nil {\n104|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n105|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(member)).WithCause(err)\n106|\t}\n107|\tif err := CheckRegularTarget(info); err != nil {\n108|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n109|\t\treturn nil, err\n110|\t}\n111|\t_ = clearNonblock(file)\n112|\treturn file, nil\n113|}\n114|\n115|// statOpenedFile stats an already-opened descriptor. It is a variable\n116|// only so seam-failure tests can force the stat-failure path through the\n117|// production openers below: fstat on a live descriptor does not fail on\n118|// any supported platform, and without the seam the error branch is\n119|// untestable. Production code never reassigns it.\n120|var statOpenedFile = func(file *os.File) (os.FileInfo, error) {\n121|\treturn file.Stat()\n122|}\n123|\n124|// OpenNoFollowDir opens an existing directory read-only without following\n125|// a trailing symlink, returning a handle for validation walks. The handle\n126|// pins the opened directory: Fstat and ReadDirnames on it observe the\n127|// directory that was opened even if its name is later retargeted.\n128|//\n129|// The Lstat fast path refuses non-directories with the same rules on\n130|// every platform; the post-open Stat below stays authoritative against a\n131|// swap between the check and the open.\n132|func OpenNoFollowDir(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n133|\tif info, err := os.Lstat(path); err != nil {\n134|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n135|\t} else if info.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n136|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target symlink\", info.Name())\n137|\t} else if !info.IsDir() {\n138|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target directory\", info.Name())\n139|\t}\n140|\tdir, err := openNoFollowDir(path)\n141|\tif err != nil {\n142|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n143|\t}\n144|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(dir)\n145|\tif err != nil {\n146|\t\t_ = dir.Close()\n147|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n148|\t}\n149|\tif info == nil || !info.IsDir() || info.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n150|\t\t_ = dir.Close()\n151|\t\tname := path\n152|\t\tif info != nil {\n153|\t\t\tname = info.Name()\n154|\t\t}\n155|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target directory\", name)\n156|\t}\n157|\treturn dir, nil\n158|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c441","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07750-3853-73c0-b383-0773367c2b50"},"sequence":123,"recorded_at":1780531400000241,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb"},"call_id":"call_01a07750717f73b1956e391e261cb361","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/errors.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"errors\"\n5|\t\"fmt\"\n6|)\n7|\n8|// Refusal sentinels. Every refusal this package produces wraps exactly one\n9|// of these, so callers match on the kind without parsing human text.\n10|var (\n11|\tErrUnsafePath = errors.New(\"secprim unsafe path\")\n12|\tErrUnsafeArgv = errors.New(\"secprim unsafe argv\")\n13|\tErrUnsafeEnv  = errors.New(\"secprim unsafe environment\")\n14|)\n15|\n16|// Error is the only refusal this package produces. Kind is one of the\n17|// sentinels above; Rule names the specification rule that fired\n18|// (for example \"member traversal\" or \"argv NUL\"); Detail names the\n19|// offending member, argument position, or variable name without echoing\n20|// foreign bytes beyond what names the site.\n21|type Error struct {\n22|\tKind   error\n23|\tRule   string\n24|\tDetail string\n25|\tcause  error\n26|}\n27|\n28|func (err *Error) Error() string {\n29|\tif err.cause == nil {\n30|\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%v: %s: %s\", err.Kind, err.Rule, err.Detail)\n31|\t}\n32|\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%v: %s: %s: %v\", err.Kind, err.Rule, err.Detail, err.cause)\n33|}\n34|\n35|// WithCause records the underlying filesystem failure for errors.Is and\n36|// errors.As without changing the refusal rendering the witnesses pin:\n37|// the cause appends after the pinned prefix.\n38|func (err *Error) WithCause(cause error) *Error {\n39|\terr.cause = cause\n40|\treturn err\n41|}\n42|\n43|func (err *Error) Unwrap() []error {\n44|\tif err.cause == nil {\n45|\t\treturn []error{err.Kind}\n46|\t}\n47|\treturn []error{err.Kind, err.cause}\n48|}\n49|\n50|// The three refusal constructors below are declared as variables so the\n51|// refusal-inventory gate can observe every exercised refusal site. Each is\n52|// the single construction site for its failure class; production code must\n53|// not build Error values any other way: no aliasing a constructor into a\n54|// second name, no &Error{} or Error{} literal, no new(Error), and no\n55|// wrapper function or method that manufactures an *Error. The inventory\n56|// test enforces every member of that shape space, not just the bare\n57|// constructor spelling.\n58|//\n59|// failPath reports a Section 16.3 path or filesystem refusal: a member\n60|// outside the contained grammar, a symlink or special file where a\n61|// regular file is required, a case-fold collision, or an unmanaged\n62|// replacement.\n63|//\n64|// failArgv reports a Section 16.4 argv refusal: an empty vector, an empty\n65|// executable or argument element, a NUL byte, or invalid UTF-8.\n66|//\n67|// failEnv reports a Section 16.7 environment refusal: a name outside the\n68|// environment-name grammar, a literal colliding with an inherited name, or\n69|// a NUL byte or invalid UTF-8 in a value.\n70|var failPath = func(rule, detail string) *Error {\n71|\treturn &Error{Kind: ErrUnsafePath, Rule: rule, Detail: detail}\n72|}\n73|\n74|var failArgv = func(rule, detail string) *Error {\n75|\treturn &Error{Kind: ErrUnsafeArgv, Rule: rule, Detail: detail}\n76|}\n77|\n78|var failEnv = func(rule, detail string) *Error {\n79|\treturn &Error{Kind: ErrUnsafeEnv, Rule: rule, Detail: detail}\n80|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
