{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6ed","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07750-3853-73c0-b383-0773367c2b50"},"sequence":465,"recorded_at":1780531400000925,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb"},"call_id":"call_01a077510157767283ae3ba796f7245b","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2ciy0s/TASK-260830-2ciy0s_review-verdict-rev2.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-2ciy0s — reviewer verdict, CR revision 2\n2|\n3|- Run: `RUN-260906-26a2e6` (reviewer/reviewer)\n4|- Change Request: `CR-TASK-260830-2ciy0s-2` rev 2, base `157a54c`,\n5|  candidate tree `703ee895c6b5f8bc2e6a97cb4aff7e538ce79c90`,\n6|  `repository_delta=present`, 33 changed paths.\n7|- Verdict: **changes requested** → `to-dev`.\n8|- `repeat-of:` **rev1/B5** (finding C3 only; C1, C2, C4 are new).\n9|- Worktree verified byte-identical to the candidate tree before and after\n10|  every probe (`git read-tree HEAD` into a temp index + `git add -A` +\n11|  `write-tree` → `703ee895c6b5f8bc2e6a97cb4aff7e538ce79c90`). Every mutant,\n12|  plant and probe file reverted; no `zz_*` or `.bak` residue in the tree.\n13|\n14|## What I re-ran myself (not accepted from the producer)\n15|\n16|| Gate | Command | Result |\n17|| --- | --- | ---: |\n18|| Full suite | `go test ./... -count=1` | exit 0, 20 packages ok |\n19|| Focused suite (post-revert) | `go test ./internal/{secprim,cliresult,provhost,scalar}` | exit 0 |\n20|| Race | `go test -race ./internal/{secprim,cliresult}` | exit 0 |\n21|| Format | `gofmt -l internal/` | clean |\n22|| Vet | `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n23|| Windows vet | `GOOS=windows go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n24|| Windows build | `GOOS=windows go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n25|\n26|Accepted from the producer without re-running: `-cover`, `tracecheck`.\n27|Everything below I executed in this session.\n28|\n29|---\n30|\n31|## Blocking findings\n32|\n33|### C1 — `Guard.Open` never binds the walk to `guard.root`; any directory handle becomes the containment root\n34|\n35|Driven through the production entry, no mocks:\n36|\n37|```\n38|NewGuard(\"<tmp>/stage\", PlatformLinux, nil)              -> ok\n39|OpenNoFollowDir(\"<tmp>/elsewhere\")                       -> handle\n40|guard.Open(thatHandle, \"secret\")                         -> ADMITTED, read \"FOREIGN\"\n41|```\n42|\n43|`openCommitRelative(root *os.File, _ string, member string, segments []string)`\n44|takes the guard root path and **discards it** (`_ string`). The unix walk starts\n45|at `root.Fd()` and never checks that the handle is the guard's root, so\n46|`Guard.Open` enforces \"relative to *a* directory\", not \"inside *this* root\". The\n47|only handle check is `root.Stat()` → `IsDir()`.\n48|\n49|This is not a caller-contract nit, because the two platforms disagree:\n50|`open_member_windows.go` uses `rootPath` as the descent base\n51|(`filepath.Join(current, segment)`, `filepath.Join(rootPath, …)`), so on Windows\n52|the same call refuses (the member is looked up under the guard root and is not\n53|there) while on unix it admits and reads. One production entry, two containment\n54|semantics, and no test on either platform drives a handle that does not match\n55|`guard.root`.\n56|\n57|`Guard`'s own doc calls the type \"a containment boundary … a validated absolute\n58|root\", and `Resolve` enforces that root. `Open` — the half that is supposed to be\n59|the commit conjunct of §16.3 containment — does not.\n60|\n61|Named fix: bind the handle to the root. Either `fstat` the handle and compare\n62|`Dev`/`Ino` against a `Stat` of `guard.root` (refusing on mismatch with a named\n63|rule), or remove the choice by exposing `guard.OpenRoot() (*os.File, error)` and\n64|taking the handle only from there. Drive the mismatched-handle refusal as a\n65|negative test on both platforms, and make the unix and Windows walks agree on\n66|what the root is.\n67|\n68|### C2 — a symlinked intermediate at depth ≥ 2 is misclassified, because `classifyCommitErr` fstats a closed descriptor\n69|\n70|Measured through `Guard.Open` against a real filesystem:\n71|\n72|| Member | Symlinked component | Refusal |\n73|| --- | --- | --- |\n74|| `hop1/loot` | 1st intermediate | `member symlink escape` |\n75|| `l1/hop/loot` | 2nd intermediate | **`open failed: … not a directory`** |\n76|| `a/b/hop/loot` | 3rd intermediate | **`open failed: … not a directory`** |\n77|\n78|Mechanism, confirmed by moving one statement: in `openCommitRelative` the error\n79|branch closes `previous` **before** calling `classifyCommitErr(current, …)`, and\n80|from the second iteration onward `previous == current`. So `unix.Fstatat(parent,\n81|…)` runs on a descriptor that was just closed, returns `EBADF`, the symlink\n82|re-examination is skipped, and the raw `ENOTDIR` falls through to the\n83|\"open failed\" refusal. Reordering classify before close makes all three depths\n84|report `member symlink escape`:\n85|\n86|```\n87|classified := classifyCommitErr(current, segment, err)\n88|if previous >= 0 { _ = unix.Close(previous) }\n89|return nil, classified\n90|```\n91|\n92|I confirmed the errno premise on this host directly: `openat` with\n93|`O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY` on a symlink gives `ENOTDIR`, not `ELOOP`\n94|(`ELOOP=false ENOTDIR=true`), so the `fstatat` re-examination is the *only* thing\n95|that names the shape on darwin — and at depth ≥ 2 it never runs.\n96|\n97|Consequences:\n98|\n99|1. `README.md` claims `Guard.Open` \"refuses a symlinked parent at commit time as\n100|   `member symlink escape`\". Falsified for every member with two or more\n101|   intermediate components.\n102|2. It is a use-after-close on a file descriptor. Every branch still refuses, so\n103|   this is not an escape — but in a concurrent process the fd number can be\n104|   reused between the close and the `Fstatat`, and the check then interrogates an\n105|   unrelated directory. A refusal that consults a recycled descriptor is not a\n106|   refusal anyone can reason about.\n107|3. The suite cannot see it: every committed commit-walk case uses a one- or\n108|   two-segment member (`sub/loot`, `final-link`, `ok/absent`, `nodir/file`), so\n109|   `previous` is always `-1` and the closed-fd path is never taken. The same\n110|   ordering bug exists on the final-component open (`previous` closed before\n111|   `classifyCommitErr`); it is masked there because `ELOOP` catches a trailing\n112|   symlink before the `fstatat`.\n113|\n114|Named fix: classify before closing at both sites, and add a commit-walk case with\n115|at least three segments and the symlink on a non-first intermediate.\n116|\n117|### C3 — the inventory gate closed four spellings, not the class (`repeat-of: rev1/B5`)\n118|\n119|Round 1: four of four bypass plants walked through an **identifier-keyed**\n120|gate. Round 2 rebuilt the derivation around alias fixpoints, literals,\n121|`new(Error)`, factories and value escapes — and keyed it on the *type*\n122|identifier instead. I planted a fifth shape the gate does not name; it and two\n123|variants walk through a fully green suite. Control proves the live instrument\n124|works:\n125|\n126|| Plant | Shape | Result |\n127|| --- | --- | ---: |\n128|| CONTROL | `failArgv(\"plant control rv\", …)`, unexercised | **CAUGHT** — `refusal site plant_control_rv.go:4 never fired during the suite` |\n129|| **H** | `type shadowAlias = Error` then `&shadowAlias{Kind: ErrUnsafeArgv, …}` | **BYPASSED** |\n130|| **I** | `type shadowNamed Error` then `(*Error)(&shadowNamed{…})` | **BYPASSED** |\n131|| **J** | `func plantJFactory(…) *shadowResult` returning `&shadowResult{…}` | **BYPASSED** |\n132|\n133|Root cause: `isErrorLiteral` matches `*ast.Ident` name `== \"Error\"` and\n134|`returnsError` matches `*ast.StarExpr` → `Ident{\"Error\"}`. Any other spelling of\n135|the same type is invisible, so an alias-spelled literal is neither a literal\n136|stray nor inside a factory, and an alias-spelled result makes the enclosing\n137|function not a factory either. H is a direct, invisible violation of the rule\n138|`errors.go` states, exactly as rev1's plant B was.\n139|\n140|`README.md` says the inventory \"additionally fails on any refusal built outside\n141|the constructors (aliases, literals, factories, value escapes)\". \"Any\" is\n142|falsified.\n143|\n144|This is the second consecutive round of the same class, so the recommendation is\n145|a gate rather than a third syntactic revision:\n146|\n147|- **Preferred: state the bound.** Keep the enumerated-spelling gate and declare\n148|  in `errors.go`, the derivation comment and the README that the witness covers\n149|  the `Error` identifier spelling, and that alias or named-type spellings of the\n150|  type are outside it. That is honest, costs one paragraph, and stops the\n151|  find-a-sixth-shape loop.\n152|- **Or resolve types instead of syntax.** Load the package with `go/types`\n153|  (`packages.NeedTypes`) and ask whether a composite literal's type is identical\n154|  to `secprim.Error` and whether a result type is `*secprim.Error`. That closes\n155|  alias and named-type spellings in one move, and is the only version of this\n156|  gate that can claim the class.\n157|\n158|Do not ship a third round of added identifier cases and call the class closed.\n159|\n160|### C4 — the rev2 outcome artifact is truncated on the board; the AC ratio and the survivor/withdrawal reasoning are not there\n161|\n162|`TASK-260830-2ciy0s_secprim-rev2.md` on the board is 12 515 bytes and ends\n163|mid-sentence:\n164|\n165|```\n166|### Survivors with stated bounds — 2\n167|\n168|- N7 (drop NamedPipe disjunct only): SURVIVES — the `!mode.IsRegular()`\n169|  catch-all subsum\n170|...[truncated 5836 chars]\n171|```\n172|\n173|Everything after that is gone from the persisted evidence: the AC-row coverage\n174|table (the reported 16 of 25 and the production call site per row), the R12\n175|bound, the N24 withdrawal reasoning, M13a, and the Windows bound statement. The\n176|full text exists nowhere else — not in `.temp/`, not in another resource. The DoD\n177|requires the coverage ratio and its named call sites as *reported* evidence, and\n178|the spawn brief asked me to check the N7/R12/N24 reasoning rather than accept it;\n179|that reasoning is not checkable from the board.\n180|\n181|I re-derived what could be re-derived (below): the 41-exit denominator is exact,\n182|N7's bound is correct, and R12's is honest. But **N24 does not hold up**: I\n183|applied \"drop the directory arm only\" and it is **KILLED** by\n184|`TestCheckRegularTarget` and `TestOpenNoFollowFile` (the refusal message moves\n185|from `target directory` to `target special file`). Withdrawing it as \"invalid\"\n186|removed a valid, killing narrowing mutant from the count — under-claiming rather\n187|than over-claiming, but the stated reason is wrong and unverifiable as persisted.\n188|\n189|Named fix: attach the artifact as a file (`task-board resource add`) rather than\n190|inline `content=`, which is what truncated it, and correct the N24 row.\n191|\n192|---\n193|\n194|## Gate-by-gate results\n195|\n196|### G-A — is the walk actually relative at every step? **Yes on unix.**\n197|\n198|- **No path-string re-resolution anywhere on the unix walk.** Every step is\n199|  `unix.Openat(current, segment, …)` from the previous descriptor;\n200|  `filepath.Join` appears nowhere in `open_member_unix.go`. Intermediates carry\n201|  `O_RDONLY|O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY|O_CLOEXEC`, the final component\n202|  `O_RDONLY|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC|O_NONBLOCK`. Descriptors pin what they opened.\n203|  (Windows re-resolves the root path string; that is stated in-source as a bound\n204|  alongside compile-and-vet-only, and it is the C1 divergence.)\n205|- **Distinct grammar obligations, all refused through `Guard.Open`** — 15 of 15,\n206|  each with its own rule:\n207|\n208|  | Member | Refusal |\n209|  | --- | --- |\n210|  | `..`, `../x`, `d/../../etc/passwd` | `member grammar` |\n211|  | `/etc/passwd`, `C:/x`, `d\\f` | `member grammar` |\n212|  | `d//f` (empty segment), `d/f/` (trailing) | `member grammar` |\n213|  | `d/./f`, `./d/f`, `.` | `member grammar` |\n214|  | `\"\"` | `member grammar` |\n215|  | `d/%2e%2e/f`, `d/%252e%252e/f` | `member encoded dot` |\n216|  | `d/%c0%af/f` | `member overlong separator` |\n217|\n218|- **Final and intermediate are both driven.** Trailing symlink →\n219|  `member symlink escape` (committed `final symlink` case, re-run). Intermediate\n220|  → refused at all three depths I probed, but see C2 for the rule name.\n221|- **TOCTOU confirmed at the commit step, not by luck.** I built an honest\n222|  `stage/sub/loot`, called `Resolve` (admitted), then `rm -rf`'d `sub` and\n223|  replaced it with a symlink to `outside`, then called `Open`:\n224|  `member symlink escape`. The refusal comes from the walk, after validation had\n225|  already passed on the honest tree. No committed test drives this swap shape —\n226|  advisory only, since descriptors pin and the property holds.\n227|- **`Resolve` + `OpenNoFollowFile` still escapes** — I drove it and read\n228|  `ESCAPED`. That is expected (rev1's B1 vector through the composed API), but\n229|  `OpenNoFollowFile`'s doc comment still recommends exactly that composition\n230|  (\"Parent directories are the caller's responsibility: open the staging root\n231|  with `OpenNoFollowDir` and resolve members through a `Guard`\"). Point it at\n232|  `Guard.Open`.\n233|- **FIFO refuses through both openers.** `OpenNoFollowFile` and `Guard.Open`\n234|  (`d/pipe`) both answered `target special file: pipe` inside a 4 s deadline.\n235|  B2 closed, on the commit path too.\n236|\n237|### G-B — B7's recursive scan: **closed.**\n238|\n239|| Plant | Location | Result |\n240|| --- | --- | ---: |\n241|| CONTROL | `internal/scalar/path.go` | **CAUGHT** with path |\n242|| rev1 bypass | `internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go` | **CAUGHT** with path |\n243|| package added since | `internal/secprim/argv.go` | **CAUGHT** with path |\n244|| second nested cmd | `internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main.go` | **CAUGHT** with path |\n245|\n246|Fail-closed measured on the test's **own** arms, not just via the compiler: a\n247|plant under `internal/scalar/testdata/` (which the toolchain ignores but the walk\n248|reads) produced `emission-path walk failed closed: parse …: import path must be a\n249|string`, and a `chmod 000` file produced `emission-path walk failed closed: open\n250|…: permission denied`. An `archive/zip` import under `testdata/` is also caught,\n251|so the scan is if anything over-broad — safe direction.\n252|\n253|The file count is **derived**, not asserted: `scanned++` per file, checked only\n254|`!= 0`, and reported with `t.Logf` (110 on this tree). A new package cannot rot\n255|it.\n256|\n257|`TestNoArchiveEmissionPath` is cited by **five** `nopath` rows (Machine\n258|authentication, Live process identity, IPC/runtime, Transient locking, Mutable\n259|derived indexes). All five are now stated as archive-format bounds — each says\n260|what the scan proves and explicitly what it does not — and **none is a sole\n261|witness any more**: each row carries a second witness\n262|(`TestSecretSiteRosterIsComplete`, `axerror.TestCauseNeverReachesTheWire`, or\n263|`TestOpenNoFollowDir`). Honest.\n264|\n265|### G-C — B5's shape space: **not closed.** See C3.\n266|\n267|### G-D — B6, B3, B4: **all closed.**\n268|\n269|- **B6 corpus.** Feed and assertion are the same `forbidden` slice, one\n270|  declaration and two loops over it (`render_test.go:71-84` feeds,\n271|  `:99-103` asserts). The counts are equal *by construction*; they cannot drift\n272|  again. Both endpoints of every range are now witnessed:\n273|\n274|  | Mutant | Narrowed to admit | Result |\n275|  | --- | --- | ---: |\n276|  | `>= 0x202a` → `>= 0x202b` | U+202A | KILLED (`bidi_embedding_202a`) |\n277|  | `<= 0x202e` → `<= 0x202d` | U+202E | KILLED (`bidi_override`) |\n278|  | `<= 0x2069` → `<= 0x2068` | U+2069 | KILLED (`bidi_isolate_2069`) |\n279|  | `>= 0x2066` → `>= 0x2067` | U+2066 | KILLED (`bidi_isolate`) |\n280|  | `>= 0x200b` → `>= 0x200c` | U+200B | KILLED (`zero_width_space`) |\n281|  | drop `== 0x061c` | U+061C | KILLED (`directional_isolate_061c`) |\n282|\n283|  U+202A–U+202D are witnessed per-rune, and the range is now pinned at both ends\n284|  rather than at one.\n285|\n286|- **B3 — all six `cliresult` paths driven.** Every wiring site killed by a\n287|  narrowing mutant (`terminalLine(x)` → `x`):\n288|\n289|  | Site | Mutant | Killed by |\n290|  | --- | --- | --- |\n291|  | `Emit` success → stdout | R27 | `TestTextSuccessEmissionNeutralizes` |\n292|  | `Emit` failure → stderr | R22 | `TestTextEmissionNeutralizesHostileContent` |\n293|  | `Progress` | R23 | `TestProgressEmissionNeutralizes` |\n294|  | `Prompt` refusal text | R24 | `TestPromptRefusalCarriesNeutralizedText` |\n295|  | `Log` | R28 | `TestTextEmissionNeutralizesHostileContent` |\n296|  | `Prompt` interactive write | R28b | `TestTextEmissionNeutralizesHostileContent` |\n297|\n298|- **B4 — nil vs empty are distinct obligations.** `Env != nil` →\n299|  `len(Env) > 0` is **KILLED** by `TestExecRunnerEmptyEnvIsDenyAll`; dropping the\n300|  wiring entirely is killed by two tests. I also narrowed the co-located argv\n301|  gate two ways — stripping NUL before `CheckArgv`, and gating it on\n302|  `executable == \"\"` only — and both are **KILLED** by\n303|  `TestExecRunnerRefusesUnusableExecutable`\n304|  (`Run(\"bad\\x00bin\") = *exec.Error …, want *secprim.Error`).\n305|\n306|### G-E — the ratio and the survivors\n307|\n308|**Denominator re-derived, exact.** Running the production `deriveRefusals` over\n309|the candidate's non-test sources: **41** refusal exits — argv 5, env 9,\n310|nofollow 14, path 13 — matching the reported figure component by component, with\n311|zero strays. (Rev1's 35 plus the six new `Guard.Open` sites.)\n312|\n313|**Instruments reported separately, which is the right shape** (narrowing 27,\n314|deletion 15, instrument probes 2, bypass probe 1). Measured against the\n315|*distinct refusal rules* rather than the exit count: **14 of 28 rules carry a\n316|narrowing mutant**; the other 14 are existence-probe or fired-only. Several of\n317|those are error-propagation exits with no class boundary to move\n318|(`open failed`, `open stat failed`, `target stat missing`, `guard root handle`),\n319|but several are real class gates covered by deletion alone. I narrowed six of\n320|them myself:\n321|\n322|| Mutant | Narrowed to admit | Result |\n323|| --- | --- | ---: |\n324|| `env value NUL`, allowlist arm → prefix-only | NUL past index 0 | KILLED |\n325|| `env value NUL`, literals arm → prefix-only | NUL past index 0 | KILLED |\n326|| `env value encoding`, allowlist arm → `len > 4` | short invalid UTF-8 | KILLED |\n327|| `env value encoding`, literals arm → `len > 4` | short invalid UTF-8 | KILLED |\n328|| `env allowlist duplicate` → `len(name) > 3` | short duplicate names | KILLED |\n329|| `env literal collision` → `len(key) > 3` | short colliding keys | KILLED |\n330|\n331|So the gap is in the **reporting**, not the coverage: those gates are genuinely\n332|bounded and the battery understates itself. Note for the table — `env value NUL`\n333|and `env value encoding` each have two arms sharing one rule name; the derivation\n334|keys sites by `file:line`, so both arms must fire independently, and I confirmed\n335|each arm dies to its own narrowing mutant rather than hiding behind its twin.\n336|\n337|**Survivors, checked rather than accepted:**\n338|\n339|- **N7 (drop the `ModeNamedPipe` disjunct alone): SURVIVES — bound correct.**\n340|  `CheckRegularTarget`'s case list ends in `!mode.IsRegular()`, which subsumes\n341|  the device, named-pipe, socket and char-device disjuncts. Any one of them alone\n342|  is unobservable; the two-arm N7b is the right instrument, and it kills.\n343|- **R12 (drop the slash boundary from `withinRoot`): SURVIVES — bound honest,\n344|  and I verified the tripwire rather than trusting it.** I also ran the stronger\n345|  mutant (`withinRoot` → `return true`), which likewise survives, confirming the\n346|  `defensiveSites` claim that the conjunct is unreachable through the entry. The\n347|  claim that matters is that a *grammar relaxation* reddens, so I weakened the\n348|  shared grammar in `scalar.ParseRelativePath` to admit `..` segments:\n349|  - grammar weakened, `withinRoot` intact → `TestGuardPrefixCheckIsLoadBearing`\n350|    **passes**: the prefix conjunct catches the escape, which is what defense in\n351|    depth is for;\n352|  - grammar weakened **and** `withinRoot` neutralized → **FAILS**,\n353|    `Resolve(\"x/../..\") escaped to \"/stage\"`, `24 admitted members escaped the\n354|    root`.\n355|\n356|  The defensive site and its rationale are both load-bearing exactly as stated.\n357|- **N24: not a legitimate withdrawal.** See C4 — the mutant kills.\n358|\n359|### Windows — established for the tags that matter; the universal is overstated\n360|\n361|The spawn brief asked whether the reparse mapping is now established or stated as\n362|a bound. It is **established, and slightly overstated**. Checked against the\n363|toolchain in use (go1.25.5, `$GOROOT/src/os/types_windows.go`,\n364|`(*fileStat).mode()`):\n365|\n366|| Reparse tag | Go maps to | Gate outcome |\n367|| --- | --- | --- |\n368|| `IO_REPARSE_TAG_SYMLINK` | `ModeSymlink` | refused ✓ |\n369|| junction / `MOUNT_POINT`, AppExecLink, cloud placeholder (default arm) | `ModeIrregular` | refused ✓ |\n370|| `IO_REPARSE_TAG_AF_UNIX` | **`ModeSocket`** | passes the Lstat gate |\n371|| `IO_REPARSE_TAG_DEDUP` | **no type bit** (deliberately regular) | admitted |\n372|\n373|So the claim \"every other reparse point … surfaces as `ModeIrregular`, never as\n374|`ModeSymlink`\" has two counterexamples. Neither opens an escape: an AF_UNIX\n375|reparse point is refused a moment later by `CheckRegularTarget` as\n376|`target special file`, and DEDUP is a regular file by Go's explicit design\n377|decision. There is also a `GODEBUG=winsymlink=0` path (`modePreGo1_23`) under\n378|which a junction reports `ModeSymlink` instead — still refused, since the gate\n379|tests both bits. The escape-capable §16.3 \"reparse\" obligation is therefore met\n380|under both mappings; the sentence should enumerate the two exceptions and name\n381|the `GODEBUG` dependency instead of quantifying over all tags. **Advisory, not\n382|blocking.** Windows remains compile-and-vet only, never executed, which the\n383|source states.\n384|\n385|---\n386|\n387|## AC-row coverage\n388|\n389|I cannot report the ratio as measured, because the artifact that carried it is\n390|truncated (C4) and the per-row production call sites are in the missing section.\n391|What I can state from execution: the three rows rev1 falsified are now true —\n392|symlink/reparse escape at commit (driven, C2 affects the rule name not the\n393|refusal), TOCTOU dir-handle primitives (the handle is now walked, C1 affects\n394|which root it binds to), and device/FIFO/socket refusal (driven through both\n395|openers). Re-attach the table and I will check the ratio against it.\n396|\n397|---\n398|\n399|## Advisory (not blocking)\n400|\n401|1. `OpenNoFollowFile`'s doc comment still recommends the composition that\n402|   escapes containment; point it at `Guard.Open`.\n403|2. The path/containment half of `secprim` has no production caller — only\n404|   `RenderForTerminal`, `CheckArgv` and `IsEnvName` are wired\n405|   (`cliresult/output.go`, `provhost/runner.go`, `provhost/spawn.go`).\n406|   Consistent with a primitives leaf whose consumer is a later leaf, but the\n407|   README's \"a library behind the production launch and rendering paths\" reads\n408|   wider than the wiring. State which surface is wired today.\n409|3. No committed test drives the validate-then-swap TOCTOU shape. The property\n410|   holds (I drove it), so this is coverage, not correctness.\n411|4. The Windows reparse sentence — see above.\n412|\n413|## Required for revision 3\n414|\n415|1. **C1** — bind `Guard.Open`'s handle to `guard.root` (fstat/dev-ino compare, or\n416|   a `guard.OpenRoot()` that removes the choice), make the unix and Windows walks\n417|   agree on the root, and drive the mismatched-handle refusal.\n418|2. **C2** — classify before closing at both `classifyCommitErr` call sites, and\n419|   add a commit-walk case with ≥ 3 segments and the symlink on a non-first\n420|   intermediate. Correct the README sentence about the refusal rule.\n421|3. **C3** — either state the identifier-spelling bound in `errors.go`, the\n422|   derivation comment and the README, or resolve types with `go/types`. Do not\n423|   add more identifier cases and re-claim the class. Re-run plants H, I and J as\n424|   the acceptance evidence either way.\n425|4. **C4** — re-attach the outcome artifact as a file so it is not truncated, with\n426|   the AC-row table and call sites intact, and correct the N24 row (it kills).","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
