{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c7fd","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07750-3853-73c0-b383-0773367c2b50"},"sequence":601,"recorded_at":1780531400001197,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"54033564-b97c-4244-a825-33fd926ad5cb"},"call_id":"call_01a07751e3d87b0083ff0a62495e1d30","text":"Read text file `README.md`.\n(509 line(s) above)\n510|\n511|## Safe Process and Filesystem Primitives\n512|\n513|[`internal/secprim`](internal/secprim) implements the shared safe\n514|process and filesystem primitives of Sections 16.3, 16.4, and 16.7:\n515|no-follow path handling, staging containment, structured argv,\n516|environment allowlists, terminal control-string escaping, and secret\n517|redaction for free-text streams. It is a library behind the production\n518|launch and rendering paths, not a second authority: path grammars stay\n519|in [`internal/scalar`](internal/scalar) (the member gate delegates every\n520|shared rule there and adds only encoded dots, overlong separators, and\n521|the Windows alternate-stream, trailing-dot/space, and device rules the\n522|section names), byte-counted SpawnPlan rules stay in\n523|[`internal/provhost`](internal/provhost) (which delegates its\n524|environment-name grammar here so the two cannot drift), Structured\n525|Error detail redaction stays in [`internal/axerror`](internal/axerror),\n526|SSH argv admission stays in [`internal/config`](internal/config), and\n527|the `ax pane SESSION_ID` entrypoint rule stays in\n528|[`internal/terminalbackend`](internal/terminalbackend). Trust for\n529|external executables is deliberately not implemented here:\n530|`provider.trustCandidate` (Section 7.1, with the approving owner fact)\n531|and `terminalbackend.DigestFile` (Section 4.B trust tuple, with no owner\n532|fact) differ by contract, and a third path would be another copy, not\n533|convergence.\n534|\n535|`Guard.Resolve` maps one staging member to its lexical commit path under\n536|the member grammar plus an independent joined-prefix check (the prefix\n537|conjunct is unreachable through the entry and stays as defense in\n538|depth, pinned by a generated-corpus tripwire), an optional managed set,\n539|and Unicode case-fold collision detection. Lexical resolution alone\n540|does not contain: a symlinked intermediate directory redirects any\n541|path-string open outside the root, because `O_NOFOLLOW` constrains only\n542|the final component. The commit half is `Guard.Open`, which walks every\n543|component relative to an `OpenNoFollowDir` root handle (`openat` with\n544|`O_NOFOLLOW` per component on unix, never re-resolving a path string)\n545|and refuses a symlinked parent at commit time as `member symlink\n546|escape`. `OpenNoFollowFile`/`OpenNoFollowDir` open without following a\n547|trailing symlink (`O_NOFOLLOW` on unix; an Lstat gate refusing symlinks\n548|and irregular reparse points with a stated check-then-open TOCTOU bound\n549|on Windows) and re-verify shape on the descriptor; the unix open sets\n550|`O_NONBLOCK` so a FIFO refuses instead of stalling the opener.\n551|`Command` builds only from argv arrays — the package has no shell\n552|constructor, proven by a token scan plus a behavioral no-`os/exec`\n553|import gate that catches the token-preserving mutant.\n554|`BuildEnv` builds the exact child environment from an allowlist plus\n555|disjoint literals (absent names skipped, never defaulted), wired into\n556|`provhost.ExecRunner.Env`: nil inherits, an empty list denies all —\n557|the two are tested as distinct obligations. `EscapeForTerminal`\n558|neutralizes ANSI/OSC, C0/C1 controls, DEL, bidi overrides and isolates,\n559|directional marks, zero-width characters, and invalid UTF-8 while\n560|keeping newline, tab, and legitimate text (idempotent, so double-wiring\n561|is safe), wired into all six `cliresult` text paths (`Log`, `Progress`,\n562|interactive `Prompt`, the non-interactive refusal text, and both `Emit`\n563|directions, each pinned by a hostile-content test) with JSON mode left\n564|byte-exact. `Redact` scrubs caller-known secrets, private-key blocks,\n565|sensitive `key=value` pairs, and URL userinfo; Section 16.2 claims no\n566|reliable content-level scrubbing and neither does this package — the\n567|bound is stated on the function. The secret-site census derives every\n568|secret-named declaration, parameter, tag, and literal across the\n569|secret-handling packages from syntax and requires a disposition row\n570|with a verified witness for each (names outside the 13-token\n571|vocabulary are outside the witness, stated as a bound), the Section\n572|16.2 class roster derives all nine exclusion classes from the pinned\n573|document the same way, and the five archive-`nopath` rows claim only\n574|the bound the recursive import scan proves (no archive-writer import\n575|anywhere under `internal/`, nested packages included — not the absence\n576|of every non-archive emission shape). The refusal inventory derives all\n577|41 constructor call sites and additionally fails on any refusal built\n578|outside the constructors (aliases, literals, factories, value\n579|escapes). Windows behavior is compile-and-vet only, never executed;\n580|the Lstat reparse mapping is established from the Go runtime source\n581|(`ModeSymlink` only for true symlinks, every other reparse point\n582|surfacing as `ModeIrregular`, which the gate also refuses). The package\n583|mutates no durable state and keeps no cache, so it has no crash or\n584|idempotency surface.\n585|\n586|Run the focused tests and coverage with:\n587|\n588|```bash\n589|go test ./internal/secprim -count=1\n590|go test ./internal/secprim -cover -count=1\n591|```\n592|\n593|## Canonical JSON and Immutable Object Identities\n594|\n595|[`internal/canonicaljson`](internal/canonicaljson) exposes the production RFC\n596|8785 JCS entry point and the AX immutable-object identity calculation. The JCS\n597|path validates I-JSON input before canonicalization, including duplicate-name,\n598|UTF-8, surrogate-pair, ECMAScript number-formatting, string-escaping, and\n599|UTF-16 property-ordering rules. Its number behavior is checked against every\n600|finite RFC 8785 Appendix B sample through the production entry point.\n601|\n602|All four Section 1.6 boundary fixtures that publish a digest are pinned:\n603|`NUM-SAFE-MAX`, `NUM-U64-STRING`, `NUM-U64-MAX`, and `JCS-UTF16-ORDER` are each\n604|recomputed from the production encoder and compared against the value quoted\n605|from `SPEC.md` at the pinned commit, not against a value the implementation\n606|derived for itself.\n607|\n608|The `utf8.ValidString` re-checks in `closed_shapes.go` are kept and documented\n609|rather than deleted; each names `decodeStrict` as the validator that subsumes it.\n610|That subsumption is machine-checked, not only asserted in prose: no exported\n611|function or method may hand an already-decoded value to one of those re-checks,\n612|and `decodeStrict` must remain the only place in the package where bytes become\n613|Go values. Adding a second decoder reddens the pin, as does a map-taking\n614|exported entry point that reaches a re-check by calling it, through the\n615|package's own `immutableObjectShapeValidators` dispatch table, or as a method on\n616|an exported type. All 7 of the 7 derived re-check functions are reachable from\n617|an exported entry point, so none of them is pinned vacuously, and that ratio is\n618|itself asserted rather than left to prose.\n619|\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
